Third-party Rust crates
-----------------------

The interactive interface is built on Ratatui and Crossterm; provider requests
go through reqwest with rustls; serde and serde_json handle the pi-compatible
JSON formats; the remaining crates (sha2, hmac, rsa, base64, flate2, tar,
time, unicode-width, unicode-segmentation, url, uuid) cover hashing, signing,
archives, and text measurement. All of them are fetched from crates.io as
recorded in Cargo.lock rather than vendored here. Provider wire protocols, SSE
parsing, AWS SigV4 and the event-stream framing, and the cross-process lock
are GoshCoder's own code on top of those crates.

A Rust binary statically links its dependencies, so a release archive has to
carry their notices. `make dist` collects the licence text of every crate that
survives into the build — for that platform specifically, from `cargo
metadata --filter-platform` rather than from Cargo.lock as a whole — into a
`licenses/` directory beside the binary, with an index listing each crate and
version. A crate with no discoverable licence fails the build rather than
shipping silently; see scripts/collect-licenses.sh.

Building from source instead? The same texts are in your Cargo registry
cache, under the source directories `cargo metadata` reports for each
package. Cargo.lock is not a substitute: it records versions and checksums,
not licences.

A few crates declare plain SPDX `MIT` in their manifest but ship no licence
file of their own. For those the standard MIT text recorded under
scripts/license-exceptions/MIT/ is carried in the archive, so the notice is
auditable rather than inferred.


